<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jan Vansina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jan Vansina was a Belgian historian and anthropologist who pioneered the use of oral tradition as a source for African history. His fieldwork among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Kuba</a> of the Congo and in Rwanda in the 1950s led to studies such as Oral Tradition (1961) and The Children of Woot, a history of the Kuba. He taught for many years at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a>–Madison and helped establish African history as an academic discipline.</p></div>
